1. Use a Dedicated Data Layer

Keep network logic isolated in a Repository or DataSource class.

Don’t make API calls directly from ViewModel or Composables.

2. Use Retrofit with OkHttp

Retrofit is the standard library for REST APIs in Android.

OkHttp handles connections, timeouts, and logging.

3. Use Dependency Injection (Hilt / Koin)

Inject your Retrofit, OkHttpClient, and Repository instances.

This makes testing easier and prevents tight coupling.

4. Handle Network Errors Gracefully

Wrap API responses in a sealed class to represent success, error, and loading.

5. Use Coroutines for Asynchronous Calls

Avoid enqueue() callbacks — use suspend functions.

ViewModel should launch network calls inside viewModelScope.launch.

6. Use DTOs and Mappers

Don’t expose raw API models to the UI.

Create DTOs (Data Transfer Objects) and map them to your domain models.

7. Add Interceptors for Logging and Headers

Use HttpLoggingInterceptor for debugging.

Add common headers (like authorization tokens) with custom interceptors.

8. Avoid Hardcoding URLs and Keys

Store BASE_URL and API keys in local.properties or BuildConfig.
Never commit API keys to GitHub.

9. Implement Caching

Use OkHttp caching or Room for offline support.

